    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with a clear emphasis on exploiting favorable matchups against struggling starters. **Max Muncy** (67.46) and **Henry Bolte** (65.39) headline our top options, both benefiting immensely from the soft matchup against Aldegheri (5.54 xERA). **Bolte** is particularly intriguing as a leadoff hitter with a massive 10% order boost, while **Muncy** leverages a strong platoon advantage and wind conditions favoring deep flies. **James Wood** (64.47) remains our most potent offensive engine despite a looming 30% rain risk; his combination of the leadoff spot, a strong park factor (+16%), and elite sprint speed makes him an auto-start, assuming the game clears the weather hurdle.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved the final utility spot, where **Kyle Karros** (55.11) barely edged out **Alex Freeland** (48.60). While **Freeland** showed decent upside, his projected spot at the bottom of the order (-8%) dragged his efficiency score below our threshold, whereas **Karros** secured the start by maintaining a consistent floor against Prielipp. We are also closely monitoring the pending status of **Dalton Rushing**, **Austin Riley**, and **Jose Caballero**; if **Rushing** drops below the #8 hole or if **Caballero** faces a shift-heavy defensive alignment, we may see their efficiency scores dip further. \n\nManagerial caution is advised regarding **Corbin Carroll** (57.59) and **Seiya Suzuki** (48.05), who are both battling difficult matchups against high-caliber arms in Woodruff and Rasmussen. While **Carroll** provides necessary speed, his park factor (-6%) and SP skill penalty (-14%) mean his production relies entirely on his ability to disrupt the basepaths rather than raw power. Meanwhile, **Tyler Soderstrom** remains on the bench due to his status as a non-starter, and we have opted to sit **Zach McKinstry** entirely, as his efficiency score of 39.84 failed to clear our mandated 40.0 \"Zebras Floor,\" prioritizing the roster's overall category stability over his marginal output.",
